
  The 1970s and 1980s saw hash functions grow more sophisticated. Early functions were simple modular
  arithmetic; later work emphasized universal hashing, introduced by J. Lawrence Carter and Mark N. Wegman
  in 1979, which showed that choosing a hash function randomly from a family guarantees good average-case
  behavior against any adversarial input. This idea underpinned modern hash function design and, later,
  cryptographic concerns.

  The 1990s reoriented hash tables for practical systems. Dynamic resizing—rehashing to grow the
  table—became standard, giving amortized O(1) insertion. Languages absorbed the structure into their
  cores: Perl's hashes, Python's dictionaries, Java's HashMap, Ruby's hashes, and C++11's
  std::unordered_map. Each implementation made trade-offs between memory, speed, and iteration order.

  A major security crisis arrived in 2003, when researchers showed that algorithmic complexity attacks
  could force hash collisions, degrading O(1) operations to O(n). Attackers flooded web servers with
  specially crafted parameter names, exhausting CPU. The response reshaped the field: languages adopted
  randomized and keyed hash functions, most notably SipHash, introduced by Jean-Philippe Aumasson and
  Daniel J. Bernstein in 2012, which became the default for Python, Ruby, and Rust.

  The most recent chapter is open-addressing revival. SwissTable, developed at Google around 2017 and
  released as C++'s absl::flat_hash_map, uses a control byte array and SIMD probing to achieve
  cache-friendly, extremely fast lookups. Rust's hashbrown and Python's compact dict layout follow similar
  principles.

  Today the hash table is nearly ubiquitous—powering compilers, databases, caches, and language runtimes.
  From Luhn's 1953 memo to SwissTable's SIMD probes, its history is a lesson in how a simple idea, refined
  through decades of theory and engineering, becomes infrastructure no one thinks about.

